<!-- SECTION 6 -->
<h1>6. User Roles &amp; What Each Role Can Do</h1>
<p>Access control is enforced at the top of every PHP file. If a user's role is not in the allowed list, they are immediately redirected to login.</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Role</th><th>Badge Colour</th><th>Represents</th><th>What They Can Do</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>ADMIN</strong></td>
    <td>Red</td>
    <td>System Administrator (IT admin or Adjutant's assistant)</td>
    <td>Everything: create/manage users, manage companies and platoons, manage nominal roll, approve attendance and leave, view all reports, change notification settings</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>ADJT_SA</strong></td>
    <td>Blue</td>
    <td>Adjutant or Subedar Adjutant</td>
    <td>View and approve battalion parade state, view manpower shortages, duty overview, access all reports. Cannot enter daily attendance or manage users.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>CHM_CLERK</strong></td>
    <td>Olive Green</td>
    <td>Company Havildar Major or Company Clerk</td>
    <td>Enter daily attendance, leave, duty, course, and sick reports — <em>but only for their assigned company</em>. View their activity log. Cannot see other companies or reports.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>USER</strong></td>
    <td>Gold</td>
    <td>Commanding Officer, 2IC, Company Commanders (OCs)</td>
    <td>View-only access to parade state and reports. Cannot enter or change any data.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>How Role Checking Works in Code</h2>
<p>Every protected PHP file starts with this pattern:</p>
<pre>session_start();
if (!isset($_SESSION['role']) || !in_array($_SESSION['role'], ['ADMIN', 'ADJT_SA'])) {
    header("Location: ../auth/login.php");
    exit();
}</pre>
<p>The allowed roles array changes per file. For example, <code>daily_attendance.php</code> only allows <code>CHM_CLERK</code>, while <code>manage_users.php</code> only allows <code>ADMIN</code>.</p>

<h2>Clerk Company Restriction</h2>
<p>For clerks, their company restriction is enforced via the session variable:</p>
<pre>$clerkCompanyId = $_SESSION['company_id'];
// All queries include:
WHERE p.company_id = $clerkCompanyId</pre>
<p>This ensures a clerk from A Company can never see or enter data for B Company soldiers.</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- SECTION 7 -->
<h1>7. How the Application Works (Step-by-Step Workflows)</h1>

<h2>Daily Morning Workflow (The Core Use Case)</h2>

<h3>Step 1: Company Clerk Logs In (0700–0800 hrs)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Clerk opens browser, goes to <code>http://localhost/BN_Parade_State_Automation</code></li>
  <li>Clicks "Login", enters username and password</li>
  <li>System verifies credentials (bcrypt), creates session, redirects to Dashboard</li>
</ol>

<h3>Step 2: Clerk Enters Daily Attendance</h3>
<ol>
  <li>From dashboard, clerk clicks "Company Entries" → "Daily Attendance"</li>
  <li>Date defaults to today. A table shows all soldiers in the clerk's company</li>
  <li>For each soldier, the clerk selects one status from the dropdown (Present, Leave, Course, etc.)</li>
  <li>For non-Present statuses, clerk types a remark (e.g., leave station, hospital name)</li>
  <li>At the bottom, a live counter shows totals by status (e.g., "Present: 45, Leave: 3, Sick: 2")</li>
  <li>Clerk clicks "Save Attendance" — records saved to database with <code>approval_status = 'Pending'</code></li>
</ol>

<h3>Step 3: Clerk Enters Other Records (If Applicable)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Leave Entry: formal leave application for any soldier going on leave</li>
  <li>Duty Roster: assign soldiers to Guard, Sentry, or QRT duties</li>
  <li>Course Entry: log soldiers attending training courses</li>
  <li>Sick Report: document soldiers reported sick</li>
</ul>

<h3>Step 4: Admin Approves Attendance</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Admin logs in, Dashboard shows "Pending Approvals: X"</li>
  <li>Goes to Approvals → Approve Attendance</li>
  <li>Reviews each pending record, clicks Approve or Reject</li>
  <li>Approved records are locked — clerks can no longer edit them</li>
</ol>

<h3>Step 5: Adjutant Compiles &amp; Approves Parade State</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Adjutant logs in after approval, goes to Parade State Control → Battalion Parade State</li>
  <li>System shows auto-compiled company-wise summary (based on approved attendance)</li>
  <li>Adjutant verifies totals and clicks "Approve Parade State"</li>
  <li>State is locked and archived with today's date</li>
</ol>

<h3>Step 6: CO Views Parade State</h3>
<ol>
  <li>CO (USER role) opens the portal and views the approved parade state</li>
  <li>Sees total battalion strength: present, absent, leave, course, sick, etc.</li>
  <li>Can print or download PDF for the daily orders file</li>
</ol>

<h2>How the Parade State Is Calculated</h2>
<p>The system queries the attendance table and maps each status to a row in the official parade state format:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Attendance Status</th><th>Parade State Row</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Present</td><td>Total Present</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Absent</td><td>AWL/OSL (Absent Without Leave)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Leave</td><td>AL or CL row (based on leave type)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Course</td><td>Course/Cadre row</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sick Report / MH</td><td>MH (Military Hospital) row</td></tr>
  <tr><td>TD (Temporary Duty)</td><td>Temp Duty / Att Duty / Fwd/Op Area (based on remarks)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Duty</td><td>Counted under In Unit (present for strength)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Attached Out</td><td>Posting Out / Local Att row</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Other</td><td>Other Out row</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Password Reset Workflow (Self-Service)</h2>
<ol>
  <li>User clicks "Forgot Password" on login page</li>
  <li>Enters username</li>
  <li>System fetches and displays their stored security question</li>
  <li>User types their answer — system verifies against bcrypt hash</li>
  <li>If correct, user enters and confirms new password</li>
  <li>New bcrypt hash saved to database</li>
  <li>Redirected to login with success message</li>
</ol>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- SECTION 8 -->
<h1>8. Security Features</h1>
<p>This section explains every security measure built into the system, and how it works.</p>

<h2>1. Password Hashing (bcrypt)</h2>
<p>Passwords are never stored in plain text. PHP's built-in bcrypt is used:</p>
<pre>// Storing a password:
$hash = password_hash($plaintext, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);

// Verifying a password:
if (password_verify($inputPassword, $storedHash)) { ... }</pre>
<p>Even if someone reads the database, they cannot reverse the hash to get the original password.</p>

<h2>2. CSRF Protection (Cross-Site Request Forgery)</h2>
<p>Every form in the system includes a hidden CSRF token. This prevents malicious websites from submitting forms on a user's behalf:</p>
<pre>// Generate token (in includes/csrf.php):
$_SESSION['csrf_token'] = bin2hex(random_bytes(32));

// In HTML form:
&lt;input type="hidden" name="csrf_token" value="&lt;?= $_SESSION['csrf_token'] ?&gt;"&gt;

// On POST — verify before processing:
if (!hash_equals($_SESSION['csrf_token'], $_POST['csrf_token'])) { die("CSRF"); }</pre>

<h2>3. Rate Limiting (Login Brute Force Protection)</h2>
<p>After 5 failed login attempts, the account is locked for 15 minutes. State is stored in a temp JSON file keyed by username. This prevents automated password-guessing attacks.</p>

<h2>4. SQL Injection Prevention (Prepared Statements)</h2>
<p>All database queries use MySQLi prepared statements. User input is never directly interpolated into SQL strings:</p>
<pre>// Safe — user input goes into '?' placeholder:
$stmt = mysqli_prepare($conn, "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?");
mysqli_stmt_bind_param($stmt, "s", $username);
mysqli_stmt_execute($stmt);</pre>

<h2>5. XSS Prevention (Output Escaping)</h2>
<p>All data from the database or user input that is displayed in HTML is passed through <code>htmlspecialchars()</code> to prevent script injection:</p>
<pre>echo htmlspecialchars($row['full_name'], ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');</pre>

<h2>6. Session Security</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Session ID is regenerated on login (<code>session_regenerate_id(true)</code>) to prevent session fixation attacks</li>
  <li>Session is completely destroyed on logout</li>
  <li>All protected pages check session role at the very top before any output</li>
</ul>

<h2>7. Security Questions for Password Reset</h2>
<p>Security question answers are also bcrypt-hashed — they are treated with the same security as passwords. A compromised database cannot be used to reset passwords without knowing the answer.</p>

<h2>8. Approval Workflow as Data Integrity Layer</h2>
<p>The approval workflow is not just a business process — it is a security control. Once attendance is approved, it cannot be modified by the clerk. This prevents after-the-fact data manipulation.</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- SECTION 9 -->
<h1>9. UI/UX Design</h1>

<h2>Design Philosophy</h2>
<p>The UI is designed to feel official, professional, and military-appropriate. The colour palette is inspired by the Indian Army — deep navy, saffron/gold accents, and olive tones for operational data.</p>

<h2>Colour Palette</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Colour</th><th>Hex</th><th>Used For</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Deep Navy (Primary)</td><td>#0b1a2e</td><td>Page background (dark mode), headers</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Saffron/Gold (Accent)</td><td>#c9a227</td><td>Borders, highlights, buttons, news ticker</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Red (Admin)</td><td>#c62828</td><td>ADMIN role badge</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Blue (Adjutant)</td><td>#1565c0</td><td>ADJT_SA role badge</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Olive Green (Clerk)</td><td>#558b2f</td><td>CHM_CLERK role badge</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Amber/Gold (User)</td><td>#f57f17</td><td>USER role badge</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Light (Background)</td><td>#f9f9f9</td><td>Page background (light mode)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Dark Mode / Light Mode Toggle</h2>
<p>Users can switch between dark and light themes using a sun/moon icon button in the top navigation bar. The preference is stored in the browser's <code>localStorage</code> under the key <code>bnps_theme</code> — so it persists between sessions without needing a database entry.</p>
<pre>// JavaScript in head_meta.php:
const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem('bnps_theme') || 'dark';
document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', savedTheme);

// CSS uses CSS variables per theme:
[data-theme="dark"] { --bg-color: #0b1a2e; --text-color: #e5e5e5; }
[data-theme="light"] { --bg-color: #f9f9f9; --text-color: #2c3e50; }</pre>

<h2>Typography</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Fira Sans</strong> — Used for headings, labels, navigation. Clean and professional.</li>
  <li><strong>Fira Code</strong> — Monospace font for all numeric data (attendance counts, strength figures, Army numbers). Makes numbers easier to read and align in tables.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Page Layout Patterns</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Page Type</th><th>Layout Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Homepage (index.php)</td><td>2-column layout: left = image carousel + Army commands grid; right = news, notices, links cards</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>Stat cards row at top; module cards grid below (responsive 3–4 columns)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Data Entry Pages</td><td>Topbar + left sidebar + main content area with form or table</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Report Pages</td><td>Topbar + full-width data table with print/export buttons</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Navigation Structure</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Topbar:</strong> Brand logo on left, user name + role badge in centre-right, Profile and Logout buttons on right</li>
  <li><strong>Sidebar:</strong> Role-specific navigation links with SVG icons; active page is highlighted</li>
  <li><strong>Breadcrumbs:</strong> Every internal page shows its location path (e.g. Dashboard › Clerk › Daily Attendance)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Toast Notifications</h2>
<p>Success and error messages appear as toast pop-ups in the top-right corner of the screen. They auto-dismiss after 4.5 seconds or can be closed manually. Built with vanilla JavaScript — no library needed.</p>

<h2>Responsive Design</h2>
<p>The layout adapts to mobile and tablet screen sizes using CSS flexbox and grid. Tables scroll horizontally on small screens. This allows CO-level users to check the parade state on a phone if needed.</p>

<h2>Accessibility</h2>
<ul>
  <li>ARIA roles and labels on all interactive elements</li>
  <li>Skip-to-content links at the top of every page (keyboard navigation)</li>
  <li>Semantic HTML tags: <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;article&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;header&gt;</code></li>
  <li>Sufficient colour contrast ratios in both dark and light modes</li>
  <li>Live regions for dynamic content updates (attendance counts)</li>
</ul>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- SECTION 10 -->
<h1>10. Setting Up the Project Locally</h1>

<h2>Prerequisites Checklist</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Windows PC (tested on Windows 11)</li>
  <li>XAMPP installed (Apache + MySQL + PHP 7+)</li>
  <li>A modern browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox)</li>
  <li>The project folder extracted to the correct location</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step-by-Step Setup</h2>

<h3>Step 1: Install XAMPP</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Download XAMPP from <code>apachefriends.org</code></li>
  <li>Install it — default path is <code>C:\xampp\</code></li>
  <li>Open XAMPP Control Panel, start <strong>Apache</strong> and <strong>MySQL</strong></li>
  <li>Both should show green status indicators</li>
</ol>

<h3>Step 2: Place the Project Files</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Extract the project zip or copy the folder to: <code>C:\xampp\htdocs\BN_Parade_State_Automation\</code></li>
  <li>The folder must be named exactly <code>BN_Parade_State_Automation</code></li>
</ol>

<h3>Step 3: Import the Database</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open your browser and go to <code>http://localhost/phpmyadmin</code></li>
  <li>Click the <strong>Import</strong> tab at the top (not inside any existing database)</li>
  <li>Click "Choose File" and select: <code>database/bn_full_setup.sql</code></li>
  <li>Click <strong>Go</strong></li>
  <li>Wait for success message. The database <code>bn_parade_state_db</code> is now created with all tables and sample data</li>
</ol>

<div class="warn-box">
  <strong>Important:</strong> Use <code>bn_full_setup.sql</code>, not any other SQL file, for the initial setup.
  Other files are for partial updates or re-seeding only.
</div>

<h3>Step 4: Open the Application</h3>
<ol>
  <li>In your browser, go to: <code>http://localhost/BN_Parade_State_Automation</code></li>
  <li>You should see the Infantry School homepage with the image carousel</li>
  <li>Click Login and use one of the default credentials (see next section)</li>
</ol>

<h3>Step 5: First-Time Configuration</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Log in as <code>admin</code></li>
  <li>Go to Profile → Change Password to set a secure password</li>
  <li>Create real user accounts for clerks and officers via Admin → Create User</li>
  <li>Import actual personnel data via Admin → Manage Soldiers (or via SQL)</li>
</ol>

<h2>Database Connection Details (config/db.php)</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Hostname</td><td>localhost</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Username</td><td>root</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Password</td><td>(empty string)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Database Name</td><td>bn_parade_state_db</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Character Set</td><td>utf8mb4</td></tr>
</table>
<p>To change the database password, edit <code>config/db.php</code> and update the third parameter of <code>mysqli_connect()</code>.</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

