<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- SECTION 12 -->
<h1>12. Common Errors &amp; Troubleshooting</h1>
<table>
  <tr><th>Error</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Blank white page on any PHP file</td>
    <td>PHP error with display_errors off, or missing include file</td>
    <td>In XAMPP php.ini, set <code>display_errors = On</code>. Check includes/ folder exists.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>"Connection refused" or DB error</td>
    <td>MySQL not running, or wrong DB name</td>
    <td>Start MySQL in XAMPP Control Panel. Verify <code>config/db.php</code> has correct hostname/database name.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Login always fails</td>
    <td>DB not imported, or password hashes not matching</td>
    <td>Re-import <code>bn_full_setup.sql</code>. Use exact passwords from Section 11.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Account locked (rate limiting)</td>
    <td>5+ failed login attempts</td>
    <td>Wait 15 minutes or delete the lock file in the temp directory.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Attendance not showing for clerk</td>
    <td>Clerk's company_id not set in users table</td>
    <td>Admin → Manage Users → Edit clerk → assign correct company.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>PDF download is blank</td>
    <td>No approved attendance data for selected date</td>
    <td>Ensure attendance has been entered and approved for that date before exporting.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>CSRF error on form submit</td>
    <td>Session expired or form submitted twice</td>
    <td>Reload the page and submit again. Extend session timeout in php.ini if needed.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Homepage images not loading</td>
    <td>Images/ folder missing or wrong path</td>
    <td>Ensure the <code>images/</code> folder is present in the project root.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- SECTION 13 -->
<h1>13. Glossary of Military Terms Used in the Code</h1>
<p>If you are a civilian developer encountering Army terminology for the first time, this glossary will help you understand the business domain behind the code.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning in Context</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Parade State</td><td>Daily strength report showing how many soldiers are present, absent, on leave, etc. The primary output of this system.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>BN / Battalion</td><td>The unit — typically 500–800 soldiers. Subdivided into Companies.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Company (Coy)</td><td>Sub-unit of ~100–150 soldiers. Led by a Major (OC). The system has HQ, A, B, C, D, and SP Companies.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Platoon</td><td>Sub-unit of a Company, ~30 soldiers.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section</td><td>Sub-unit of a Platoon, ~10 soldiers.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>CHM</td><td>Company Havildar Major — the senior NCO who manages company administration. The main user of the clerk module.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>ADJT / SA</td><td>Adjutant / Subedar Adjutant — officers responsible for daily administration and discipline of the battalion.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>CO</td><td>Commanding Officer — the battalion commander. Views the parade state but does not enter data.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2IC</td><td>Second-in-Command — the CO's deputy.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>OC</td><td>Officer Commanding — the company commander (a Major).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Nominal Roll</td><td>The official list of all soldiers in the unit. In code, this is the <code>personnel</code> table.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>AL / Annual Leave</td><td>30-day annual leave entitlement per year.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>CL / Casual Leave</td><td>Short-duration leave (typically up to 10 days).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>MH</td><td>Military Hospital — a soldier admitted to a military hospital.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>TD / Temporary Duty</td><td>Soldier posted temporarily to another unit or location.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>QRT</td><td>Quick Reaction Team — a duty assignment for rapid response.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>AWL / OSL</td><td>Absent Without Leave / Over Stayed Leave — unauthorised absence.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>AUTH STR</td><td>Authorised Strength — the official government-sanctioned number of soldiers a unit is allowed to have.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>POSTED</td><td>Soldiers officially assigned/posted to this unit (may differ from AUTH STR).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>ERE</td><td>Extra Regimental Employment — soldiers employed outside their battalion.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>SHAPE-1</td><td>Medical Category — fully fit for all military duties.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>AYE / BEE / CEE</td><td>Medical Categories — progressively lower fitness levels for military duties.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Army No</td><td>Service number (e.g. "15492376P") — unique identifier for each soldier across the Indian Army.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Rank (Sep, Nk, Hav, Nb Sub, Sub, Sub Maj)</td><td>Sepoy (private), Naik (corporal), Havildar (sergeant), Naib Subedar (2nd Lt equiv JCO), Subedar (Lt equiv JCO), Subedar Major (Maj equiv JCO)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>JCO</td><td>Junior Commissioned Officer — Naib Subedar, Subedar, Subedar Major.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>OR</td><td>Other Ranks — non-commissioned soldiers (Havildar and below).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>OFFR</td><td>Officer — commissioned officers (Lieutenant and above).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>SP Company</td><td>Support Company — provides specialist support (signals, logistics, medical).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Attached Out</td><td>A soldier temporarily working with another unit while remaining on this battalion's rolls.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Posted Out</td><td>A soldier permanently transferred to another unit. They should be marked inactive in the nominal roll.</td></tr>
</table>

